 "design": "R6 cross-half sensitivity, asking how much item-noise correlation would fake the observed estimate.\n\nDesign fixed before running (2026-09-15, written 20:20 EDT). The estimator assumes that item noise in\none half is independent of item noise in the other, and the coverage table shows that a cross-half\ncorrelation of 0.1 biases the estimate by -0.014 while cutting wild coverage to 0.933. The reverse\nquestion decides how much that assumption matters. With no seed covariance at all, how large would\nthe cross-half error correlation have to be before the estimator returns the observed 1.2439 on\nmargins and 1.0784 on accuracy? This run holds the true correlation at zero, sweeps the cross-half\nerror correlation over a grid from 0 to 0.5, and reports the mean estimate, its dispersion, and the\nshare of replicates at or above the observed value, with 1,000 replicates per grid point and seed\n20260919. A monotone interpolation of the mean curve gives the correlation that would reproduce each\nobserved estimate. Negative correlations are included because the bias can go either way.\n",
